Hi @Shaurya-kgen, @elevchyt, @sKopheK,

This is a known issue with CSS module ordering between Turbopack and webpack. The root cause is that **Turbopack and webpack have different algorithms for determining CSS import order**, especially when dealing with CSS modules and third-party library stylesheets.

## **Why this happens:**

1. **Import resolution order differs:** Turbopack processes CSS imports in a different order than webpack, especially when combining CSS modules with global stylesheets (like Mantine's)
2. **CSS cascade priority:** When you import Mantine's styles in `layout.tsx` and also use CSS modules, the order in which these get injected into the DOM differs between bundlers
3. **Module graph traversal:** Webpack uses depth-first traversal while Turbopack uses a different strategy, causing styles to load in different orders

## **Workarounds:**

**1. Increase specificity in your CSS modules:**

Instead of relying on cascade order, make your selectors more specific:

```scss
// subComponents.module.scss

// Instead of:
.profileMenuDropdown {
  background: white;
}

// Use higher specificity:
div.profileMenuDropdown,
:global(.mantine-Menu-dropdown).profileMenuDropdown {
  background: white;
}
```

**2. Use `!important` strategically (last resort):**

```scss
.profileMenuDropdown {
  background: white !important;
}
```

While not ideal, this ensures your styles always win regardless of order.

**3. Use Mantine's `styles` prop instead of `classNames`:**

Mantine components accept inline styles that always override:

```tsx
<Menu
  width={316}
  shadow="md"
  position="bottom-end"
  styles={{
    dropdown: {
      background: 'white',
      // your custom styles
    },
    item: {
      // ...
    }
  }}
>
```

**4. Import Mantine styles at component level instead of layout:**

Move Mantine's CSS import closer to where it's used:

```tsx
// Instead of importing in layout.tsx, import in your component
import '@mantine/core/styles.css';
import Styles from './subComponents.module.scss';
```

This gives you more control over the order.

**5. Use CSS layers (modern approach):**

If you're on a recent browser support baseline:

```scss
// Mantine styles in one layer
@layer mantine {
  @import '@mantine/core/styles.css';
}

// Your overrides in a higher-priority layer
@layer overrides {
  .profileMenuDropdown {
    background: white;
  }
}
```

Then configure layers in your global CSS:
```css
@layer mantine, overrides;
```

## **Temporary workaround:**

As @elevchyt mentioned, using webpack for now (`next dev` without `--turbopack`) will maintain consistent behavior until this is resolved.

## **For the Next.js team:**

This is a CSS ordering consistency issue that affects production builds too (as @sKopheK noted). The ideal fix would be:
1. Consistent CSS import ordering between Turbopack and webpack
2. Documentation on best practices for CSS module + third-party library styling
3. Warning when CSS order might cause unexpected results

## **Tracking:**

Related issues:
- CSS module ordering: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/64409
- Turbopack CSS handling: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/labels/Turbopack

**References:**
- https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/styling/css-modules
- https://mantine.dev/styles/styles-api/

For now, I'd recommend **workaround #3 (using Mantine's `styles` prop)** as it's the most reliable and doesn't depend on CSS order.

Let me know if any of these work for you!

You're seeing this delay because Next.js streams the layout before async metadata (like title and favicon) finishes loading.

To fix it, move your metadata logic into the generateMetadata function. It runs before streaming, so the browser gets the correct title and favicon immediately.

layout.tsx

export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic';

import { Metadata } from 'next';
import { getTenantMetadata } from '@/lib/tenant';

export async function generateMetadata({ params }): Promise<Metadata> {
  const tenant = await getTenantMetadata(params.domain);

  return {
    title: tenant?.title || 'Default Title',
    icons: {
      icon: tenant?.faviconUrl || '/default-favicon.ico',
    },
  };
}

export default function Layout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <>{children}</>;
}
Make sure your getTenantMetadata(domain) returns the title and favicon URL.

