The main difference is **when** they trigger:
- **revalidate**: 60 -> automatic, time-based
- **revalidatePath()** -> manual, on-demand

```js
// Time-based ISR – revalidates on-request after 60s (per-region)
export const revalidate = 60

// On-demand ISR – global revalidation immediately
export async function POST(request) {
  const { path } = await request.json()
  revalidatePath(path)
  return Response.json({ revalidated: true })
}
```

**Why the different treatment?**
- revalidatePath() is designed for **immediate consistency**: when you update content in a CMS or admin panel, you want all users globally to see the new content right away. It's intentionally aggressive.
- revalidate (time-based) is more about **performance optimization**:  trades consistency for performance, allowing slight staleness for faster responses and reduced server load.


**Helpful resources I found:**
- [Next.js revalidatePath API reference](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/revalidatePath)
- [Vercel on demand revalidation reference](https://vercel.com/docs/edge-cache#on-demand-revalidation)
- [Medium post](https://anjitpariyar.medium.com/revalidate-path-vs-revalidate-tag-vs-revalidate-time-next-js-v15-app-router-89db505c2f3f)

Hey! I havve faced this exact scenario with tRPC + Next.js, so here’s a pattern that works well and is quite common practice:

#### 1. **Use `getServerSideProps` for initial load**
Fetch your data via your tRPC caller and pass it down as props:

```ts
// pages/my-page.tsx
export const getServerSideProps = async (ctx) => {
  const ssg = createServerSideHelpers({ router, ctx });
  const data = await ssg.myRoute.getData.fetch();
  
  return {
    props: {
      data,
    },
  };
};
```

#### 2. **Hydrate tRPC’s useQuery with initial data**
```tsx
const { data, refetch } = trpc.myRoute.getData.useQuery(undefined, {
  initialData: props.data,
});
```

#### 3. **Use a mutation + optimistic update pattern**
```tsx
const utils = trpc.useUtils(); // v10+ caching helpers

const mutation = trpc.myRoute.updateItem.useMutation({
  onMutate: async (newData) => {
    // Cancel outgoing fetches
    await utils.myRoute.getData.cancel();

    // Snapshot previous data
    const prev = utils.myRoute.getData.getData();

    // Optimistically update cache
    utils.myRoute.getData.setData(undefined, (old) => ({
      ...old,
      ...newData,
    }));

    return { prev };
  },
  onError: (_err, _newData, context) => {
    // Rollback on error
    if (context?.prev) {
      utils.myRoute.getData.setData(undefined, context.prev);
    }
  },
  onSettled: () => {
    // Revalidate
    utils.myRoute.getData.invalidate();
  },
});
```

#### 4. **Trigger the mutation from UI**
```tsx
<button onClick={() => mutation.mutate({ id, newValue })}>
  Update
</button>
```

---

This keeps things **smooth**, **optimistic**, and aligned with your SSR data. Plus, since tRPC uses React Query under the hood, you get all the caching/revalidation magic out of the box. Voila!

