**Recommended Way: Use cache() from next/cache**
Instead of using the "use cache" directive, wrap your function with cache() like this:

```
import { cache } from 'next/cache';

const getLibraryTabs = cache(async (page: number, userId: string) => {
  const query = db
    .selectFrom("tab")
    .where("tab.authorId", "=", userId)
    .selectAll();

  const result = await execWithPagination(query, { page });
  return result;
});

```
Why this works well:

This enables response memoization based on parameters (page, userId).

It works reliably in production on both custom servers and Vercel.

It doesn’t rely on unstable experimental flags.

 **Optional: Tagging + Revalidation**
If you want to cache data and manually revalidate it later (e.g. after an update), you can use cacheTag and revalidateTag:

```
import { cache, cacheTag, revalidateTag } from 'next/cache';

const getLibraryTabs = cache(async (page: number, userId: string) => {
  cacheTag(`user-owned-tabs-${userId}`);

  const query = db
    .selectFrom("tab")
    .where("tab.authorId", "=", userId)
    .selectAll();

  const result = await execWithPagination(query, { page });
  return result;
});

```
Then later, when you update data:
```
revalidateTag(`user-owned-tabs-${userId}`);

```

