It is the native fetch, but AFAIK, the `dispatcher` option in `fetch.options` is not typed - so TS won't be happy about it. And you have to install `undici`.

```ts
import { Agent } from 'undici'

export default async function Page() {
  const response = await fetch('https://httpbin.dev/uuid', {
    dispatcher: new Agent({
      bodyTimeout: 1,
    }).compose((dispatch) => (opts, handler) => {
      console.count('HIT')
      return dispatch(opts, handler)
    }),
  })
  const uuid = await response.text()

  return <p>{uuid}</p>
}
```

This will count `HIT` every time you hit this page.

Source: https://undici.nodejs.org/#/docs/api/Dispatcher?id=example-1-basic-compose

Hi! 👋 I ran into a similar issue with custom not-found.jsx pages and metadata in Next.js App Router.

It sounds like:

You’re using a custom NotFound component.

You set a title in metadata, but it briefly appears, then disappears after render.

This seems to be a known limitation or bug in how Next.js handles metadata on error and not-found routes.

🛠️ Possible Solutions / Workarounds:
✅ 1. Use a <title> Tag Manually Inside the NotFound Component
Since the built-in metadata isn’t sticking on dynamic error pages, use the <title> tag inside your component:

tsx
Copy
Edit
import Head from 'next/head';

const NotFound = (props) => (
  <>
    <Head>
      <title>Page Not Found</title>
    </Head>
    <main>
      <h1>404 – Page Not Found</h1>
      {/* Your not found content */}
    </main>
  </>
);

export default NotFound;
This ensures the browser title doesn’t disappear.

✅ 2. Upgrade Next.js Version
You're on Next.js 15.3.2, but the latest is 15.4.2, and some bugs with metadata handling (especially not-found.tsx and layout errors) were patched.

Run:

bash
Copy
Edit
npm install next@latest
Then test again.

✅ 3. Use generateMetadata() for More Control
If your not-found.jsx needs dynamic props or async fetches, try this:

tsx
Copy
Edit
export async function generateMetadata() {
  return {
    title: 'Page Not Found',
  };
}

const Page = async () => {
  const siteData = await fetchRoute(ApiRoutes.GLOBALS);
  return <NotFound {...siteData.notFound} />;
};

export default Page;
This can override layout-level metadata issues.

###   Root Cause
- Your `not-found.jsx` is fetching data (`fetchRoute`)  
- Next.js re-renders the page after data loads  
- This re-render accidentally removes the metadata title  

##  Solutions

### Option 1: Move Data Fetching 
```jsx
// src/app/not-found.jsx
export const metadata = {
  title: 'Page Not Found',
};

// Fetch data HERE (not inside Page component)
const siteData = await fetchRoute(ApiRoutes.GLOBALS);

const Page = () => {
  return <NotFound {...siteData.notFound} />;
};

export default Page;

