Interesting issue - I did take a brief look yesterday, some notes I got from that:

The `/send-money-to-[country]/[bank].js` is a bit weird - it'd capture,`http://localhost:3000/send-money-to-foo/bar` into:

```
{
    "country": "send-money-to-foo",
    "bank": "bar"
}
``` 

And yes, `http://localhost:3000/transactions/foo`, in your setup gets captured to:

```
{
    "country": "transactions",
    "bank": "foo"
}
```

The way I understand it, when the router figures out the url-to-source-route matchers, it sees this, in this order:

- `/send-money-to-[country]/[bank].js` should match two param segments
   -  it doesn't attempt to match literal segment 1: `send-money-to-` followed by segment 2 param 
   - This is because AFAIK, Next.js params should be the entire segment `/[param]/`
   - That's why we observe `"country": "send-money-to-foo"` in the first snippet above
- `/transactions/[id].js`  should match static `transitions` followed by a param segment

Then the incoming url is matched against these, and the first one hits. 

If you were to rename `/send-money-to-[country]/[bank].js` to `/zend-money-to-[country]/[bank].js`, now the order of the matchers changes - and you'll get the right hit. This is just a quirk to understand what's going on under the hood. Not a solution proposal.

Perhaps someone else will correct me, but AFAIK, `/send-money-to-[country]/` is not necessarily supported to match the literal part of the segment + a param.

