**Proposed fix + why it happens**

`node:process` fails on the client because Webpack 5 no longer auto‑polyfills Node core modules, and the `node:` scheme isn’t resolved to browser shims by default. Next.js only replaces specific `process.env.*` at build time; it doesn’t provide a full `process` polyfill for the browser out of the box. That’s why `import 'node:process'` crashes while `process.env.X` might appear to “work”. ([[webpack](https://webpack.js.org/configuration/resolve/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)][1], [[Next.js](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/config/next-config-js/env?utm_source=chatgpt.com)][2])

**Minimal workaround (no extra bloat):**

1. Install the tiny browser shim:

```bash
npm i process
# or
yarn add process
```

2. Alias `node:process` → `process/browser` only for client builds, and provide it:

```js
// next.config.js (CJS)
module.exports = {
  webpack: (config, { isServer, webpack }) => {
    if (!isServer) {
      config.resolve.alias = {
        ...config.resolve.alias,
        'node:process': 'process/browser',
        process: 'process/browser',
      };
      config.plugins.push(
        new webpack.ProvidePlugin({
          process: ['process/browser'],
        })
      );
    }
    return config;
  },
};
```

> ESM variant:

```js
// next.config.mjs
import { ProvidePlugin } from 'webpack';

export default {
  webpack: (config, { isServer }) => {
    if (!isServer) {
      config.resolve.alias = {
        ...config.resolve.alias,
        'node:process': 'process/browser',
        process: 'process/browser',
      };
      config.plugins.push(new ProvidePlugin({ process: ['process/browser'] }));
    }
    return config;
  },
};
```

This mirrors your `NormalModuleReplacementPlugin` idea but makes the intent explicit and routes to the existing lightweight shim.

**Why not enable by default?**
Auto‑polyfilling Node core in the browser increases bundle size and is something Webpack 5 intentionally removed; frameworks typically avoid restoring it globally. A targeted alias for `node:process` keeps the fix scoped and small. ([[webpack](https://webpack.js.org/configuration/resolve/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)][1], [[GitHub](https://github.com/webpack/webpack/issues/11282?utm_source=chatgpt.com)][3])

**References**

* Webpack 5 removed Node polyfills (and recommends explicit shims). ([[webpack](https://webpack.js.org/configuration/resolve/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)][1], [[GitHub](https://github.com/webpack/webpack/issues/11282?utm_source=chatgpt.com)][3])
* Next.js replaces `process.env.*` at build time (not a full `process` polyfill). ([[Next.js](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/config/next-config-js/env?utm_source=chatgpt.com)][2])
* `node:` scheme causing `UnhandledSchemeError` is a known class of issues. ([[GitHub](https://github.com/webpack/webpack/issues/13290?utm_source=chatgpt.com)][4])

Hi @Prbarmo 

This happens because the server renders the initial HTML with empty data (`""`), and then the client replaces it with the fetched value.  
The mismatch between server and client HTML triggers the hydration warning.  

To fix it, you can:

---

### **1. Fetch data on the server**
So both server and client render the same initial content:

```jsx
export default async function Home() {
  const res = await fetch('https://example.com/api/message', { cache: 'no-store' });
  const { message } = await res.json();

  return <h1>{message}</h1>;
}
```

In Next.js 13/14 with the **App Router**, you can make your component an **async Server Component** like above, so no hydration mismatch occurs.

---

### **2. Use a loading state placeholder**
Render the same placeholder on both server and client, then update with fetched data:

```jsx
export default function Home() {
  const [data, setData] = useState('Loading...');

  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('/api/message')
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(json => setData(json.message));
  }, []);

  return <h1>{data}</h1>;
}
```

---

### **3. Suppress hydration warning**
If the content is purely client-side, mark it with `suppressHydrationWarning`:

```jsx
<h1 suppressHydrationWarning>{data}</h1>
```

This is more of a workaround; prefer server-rendering data when possible

## For protecting routes with NextAuth.js in Next.js 14 App Router, use middleware:

Create `middleware.js` in your root directory:

```javascript
import { withAuth } from "next-auth/middleware"

export default withAuth({
  callbacks: {
    authorized: ({ token }) => !!token,
  },
  pages: {
    signIn: "/login", // Custom login page (optional)
  },
})

export const config = {
  matcher: ["/dashboard/:path*"] // Add all protected routes here
}
```

This approach:
- Handles redirects at the server level before pages render
- Automatically redirects unauthenticated users to your custom `/login` page
- Provides better performance and security compared to client-side checks

**Note**: Without the `pages.signIn` config, it defaults to `/api/auth/signin`. With it, you can use your custom login page.

More info: [NextAuth.js Middleware Docs](https://next-auth.js.org/configuration/nextjs#middleware)

As of the latest update, middleware.ts has been replaced with proxy.ts (see: https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/proxy
).

The underlying logic and behavior remain the same only the file naming and structure have changed. If you were previously using middleware.ts, you can migrate the logic directly into proxy.ts without modifying the core implementation.

The best approach for protecting routes in Next.js 14 App Router with NextAuth is to use middleware. Here's the cleanest setup:

1. Create middleware.ts in the root of your project:

ts
import { auth } from "@/auth";
import { NextResponse } from "next/server";

export default auth((req) => {
  const isLoggedIn = !!req.auth;
  const isProtected = req.nextUrl.pathname.startsWith("/dashboard"); // adjust to your route

  if (isProtected && !isLoggedIn) {
    return NextResponse.redirect(new URL("/login", req.nextUrl));
  }

  return NextResponse.next();
});

export const config = {
  matcher: ["/((?!api|_next/static|_next/image|.*\\.png$).*)"],
};
2. In your auth.ts:

ts
import NextAuth from "next-auth";
import { authConfig } from "./auth.config";

export const { auth, signIn, signOut } = NextAuth(authConfig);
3. In auth.config.ts (edge-compatible, no heavy imports):

ts
import type { NextAuthConfig } from "next-auth";

export const authConfig = {
  pages: { signIn: "/login" },
  callbacks: {
    authorized({ auth, request: { nextUrl } }) {
      const isLoggedIn = !!auth?.user;
      const isProtected = nextUrl.pathname.startsWith("/dashboard");
      if (isProtected && !isLoggedIn) return false;
      return true;
    },
  },
  providers: [],
} satisfies NextAuthConfig;
Why middleware? It runs on the Edge before the page renders — no flash of protected content, and better performance than checking auth inside each page component.

For server components you can additionally call auth() directly to get the session:

ts
import { auth } from "@/auth";
const session = await auth();
if (!session) redirect("/login");
This two-file split (auth.config.ts + auth.ts) is the official pattern from the Next.js docs to keep middleware edge-compatible

