I had a similar setup (Next.js app in docs/ + Yarn workspaces packages). A couple of things helped:

Transpile local workspaces
In docs/next.config.js, make sure your internal packages are transpiled by Next (works with both Turbopack and webpack):

```js
 @type {import('next').NextConfig}
const nextConfig = {
  transpilePackages: ['package-a', 'package-b'], // your workspace packages
  // If you fall back to webpack:
  experimental: { esmExternals: 'loose' }, // optional (webpack-only)
};
module.exports = nextConfig;
```

This tells Next to bundle/transform your internal packages (replaces next-transpile-modules).
Docs: Next.js transpilePackages. 
[Next.js](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/config/next-config-js/transpilePackages?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

ESM externals & find-up
find-up is ESM-only in recent versions. If any consumer uses require('find-up'), it breaks. Use ESM or a dynamic import:

```js
// ESM usage inside your package:
import { findUp } from 'find-up';
// or dynamic:
const { findUp } = await import('find-up');
```

Alternatively, pin a CJS-compatible version of find-up as a quick test.
Background on ESM externals in Next: 
[Next.js](https://nextjs.org/docs/messages/import-esm-externals?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Turbopack vs webpack
experimental.esmExternals applies to webpack, not Turbopack. With Turbopack, make sure your workspace packages ship ESM (or are transpiled via transpilePackages) and avoid require() on ESM deps. Turbopack config reference: 
[Next.js](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/config/next-config-js/turbopack?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Package exports
In each workspace package, define a clear ESM export in package.json:

```json
{
  "name": "package-a",
  "type": "module",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/index.js"
    }
  }
}
```

(Or provide both ESM/CJS if you need compatibility.)

After adding the above, remove lockfile and reinstall to ensure links are fresh:

```
rm -rf node_modules .turbo .next
pnpm i   # or yarn / npm
```

Then next dev (Turbopack) should start without the import error; webpack + esmExternals: 'loose' should also work if you prefer webpack for now.

